{0 Entrypoints}

A {{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/rpc/}WorkerEntrypoint}
is a named class of a Worker. Its methods are RPC methods: another Worker calls
them through a service binding as if they were local functions.
{!Workero.Entrypoint} declares such a class in OCaml. JavaScript Workers and
OCaml Workers can call it.

{1 Declare a class}

Register the class under a name at module initialisation. Give the names of
its methods with [~rpc], and a function that builds the handlers:

{@ocaml[
open Workero

let () =
  Entrypoint.register "Math" ~rpc:[ "add"; "greet" ] (fun env _ctx ->
      Entrypoint.make
        ~rpc:(fun name args ->
          match (name, args) with
          | "add", [ `Int a; `Int b ] -> `Int (a + b)
          | "greet", [ `String who ] ->
              `String
                (Option.value (Env.var env "GREETING") ~default:"Hello"
                ^ ", " ^ who)
          | _ -> invalid_arg ("bad call to " ^ name))
        ())
]}

[workero build] runs the program once in [workerd] and exports every
registered class from [index.js]. It reads the names in [~rpc] at that time,
before the program has an {!Workero.Env.t}, so they are given apart from the
handler. The [rpc] handler of {!Workero.Entrypoint.make} gets the method name
and the arguments, like the [rpc] handler of a Durable Object. A call to a
declared name on a class whose handlers have no [rpc] fails with an error.

The handler gets the arguments of the call as a list of {!Workero.Rpc.t} values
and returns one. They are JSON values and also bytes, dates, big integers, maps,
sets, streams, stubs and functions; see
{{!page-durable_objects}Durable Objects}. It runs in its own fiber and may call {!Workero.Promise.await},
{!Workero.fetch} and the other bindings. An exception that the handler raises
rejects the call. The caller sees an [Error] whose message is the exception.

The runtime creates a new instance of the class for each call. The function
given to {!Workero.Entrypoint.register} runs for each of them and gets the
[env] and the [ctx], so a method can call {!Workero.Context.wait_until}. Keep no
state in it.

{1 Call it from JavaScript}

Bind the class in the [wrangler.toml] of the caller. The caller may be this
Worker or another one:

{@toml[
[[services]]
binding = "MATH"
service = "my-worker"
entrypoint = "Math"
]}

{@javascript[
const sum = await env.MATH.add(2, 3); // 5
]}

{1 Call it from OCaml}

Read the binding with {!Workero.Fetcher.of_env} and call a method with
{!Workero.Entrypoint.call}:

{@ocaml[
let handler _req env _ctx =
  let math = Fetcher.of_env env "MATH" in
  match Entrypoint.call math "add" [ `Int 2; `Int 3 ] with
  | `Int sum -> Response.text (string_of_int sum)
  | _ -> Response.text ~status:`Bad_Gateway "unexpected result"
]}

The callee may be an entrypoint written in OCaml or in JavaScript.
{!Workero.Entrypoint.call} raises {!Workero.Js_error} when the method does not
exist or raises, and when the runtime rejects the call.

{1 The fetch handler}

{!Workero.Entrypoint.make} accepts a [~fetch] handler. It answers
{!Workero.Fetcher.fetch} on a binding of the class. Without it, such a request
fails with an error.

{1 Limits}

- A value with no {!Workero.Rpc.t} form, such as an [Error], raises
  [Invalid_argument]. A result of [undefined] is [`Undefined].
- The [scheduled], [queue], [email] and [tail] handlers of a
  class are not supported.
- A class cannot share its name with a Durable Object class, and the method
  names [constructor], [prototype], [__proto__], [then], [env], [ctx], [fetch],
  [scheduled], [queue], [email], [tail], [trace], [tailStream], [test],
  [connect] and [dup] are reserved.
- RPC methods on Durable Object stubs are not part of this page.
